From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] GS CPU backend: four texels per format dispatch, constant fills written directly

What it is for:
  In Ben 10: Protector of Earth's first level the GS is the limit. On a
  replay of the level's GS commands (perf), reading texels for the
  bilinear filter (the SampleTexture lambda, GSMem::ReadTexture,
  wrapTextureCoordinate) was about 30% of the time, and untextured
  sprites (mostly buffer clears) went through the full WritePixel path
  for every pixel.

The changes:
  - SampleTexture: the bilinear path wraps u0, u1, v0, v1 once each
    (8 calls before), reads the four texels with GSMem::ReadTexture4 (one
    format dispatch, the same reads in the same order through the texture
    page cache), and checks the palette key once per sample instead of
    once per texel (the key is the same for the four texels, so the
    palette cache is reset exactly when it was before).
  - DrawSprite, untextured: constantPixelWrites follows WritePixel for
    the case where nothing depends on memory already written (no frame
    read: no blending, FBMSK, preserved destination alpha or destination
    alpha test with a stored alpha; Z test NEVER or ALWAYS). Then every
    pixel gets the same frame and Z values: they are worked out once and
    written pixel by pixel, frame then Z, as WritePixel writes them.
  Same results bit for bit.

How it was checked:
  The 15 s GS replay of the first level (the project's tools/gs_replay,
  findings/20 s.23) gives the same GS memory hashes at all 35 points and
  at the end on GSCpuBackend; best of 3 runs, two rounds: 13.5-14.4 s ->
  12.1-12.4 s. ps2xTest 454/454.

